.TH BIGNUMS 7 "2026-09-25" "BigNums 3" "Library Functions Manual"
.nr HY 0
.ds AD l
.SH NAME
bignums \- dynamic signed integers and binary floating point
.SH DESCRIPTION
BigNums is an experimental C99 library with dynamically allocated 32-bit limbs,
exact integer arithmetic and explicitly controlled binary float precision.
Integers use a separate sign with unsigned magnitude limbs and canonical
positive zero. Parsing accepts a leading + or -, and arithmetic handles both
signs. Integer scalar division and right shifts truncate toward zero;
scalar remainders have the dividend's sign. Bit lookup reads magnitude bits.
Numbers grow subject to addressable/indexable storage and allocation success.
Float exponent range remains signed 32-bit. There are no NaN/infinity values.
.SH BUILD
.nf
make -C bigNums shared
make -C bigNums check demo
\&./build/demo
.fi
.PP
The build needs GNU Make, a Linux/ELF C99 toolchain and libm.
C failure tests require GNU-style linker --wrap.
.SH INSTALLATION
Run ./build.sh to install into $HOME/.local or the PREFIX environment variable.
For example:
.nf
cc app.c -I"$HOME/.local/include" -L"$HOME/.local/lib" \\
    -Wl,-rpath,"$HOME/.local/lib" -lbignums -o app
.fi
.PP
\&./build.sh --system builds first, then uses sudo unless already root to install
into /usr/local, register /usr/local/lib and run ldconfig. It requires
Linux/glibc and accepts no path overrides. Prefix-based installs do not invoke
sudo or update the loader cache. PREFIX, LIBDIR, INCLUDEDIR, MANDIR and DESTDIR
support custom/staged installations. Direct make install defaults to /usr/local.
The shared library records its own libm dependency.
.SH LIFETIME
.nf
BigInt n = BIGINT_INIT;
int rc = bigIntFactorial(&n, 5000);
if(!rc) { rc = printBigInt(&n); }
bigIntDestroy(&n);
.fi
.PP
Initialize every destination, deep-copy using Copy, and free owned storage
using Destroy on every exit path. Structure assignment is not a value copy.
Zero retains capacity. See bignums(3) for invariants and ABI migration.
.SH PRECISION
Floats default to 1024 significant 32-bit limbs, configurable with
bigFloatSetPrecision. Capacity is independent. Add/Sub/Mul use destination
precision; Div/Reciprocal/Sqrt use their explicit positive precision and store
it on success. Arithmetic rounds toward zero. Decimal conversion rounds to
nearest with ties away from zero and allocates output dynamically.
.SH ALGORITHMS
Verified FFT multiplication uses runtime coefficient and transform-length
bounds; unsupported sizes fall back to exact schoolbook multiplication.
Schoolbook multiplication, binary division and restoring square root retain
quadratic scaling. Dynamic allocation removes fixed storage limits, not these
algorithmic costs. Arithmetic scratch limbs are heap allocated.
.SH ABI
SONAME is libbignums.so.3. Recompile and migrate callers of earlier versions.
The signed layout and scalar remainder types differ from ABI 2; lifetime
requirements also differ from ABI 1. Float mantissas must remain nonnegative.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bignums (3),
.BR bigIntMul (3),
.BR bigFloatSetPrecision (3),
.BR bigFloatDiv (3),
.BR fft (3)
